{"schema":"clawhub.skill.verify.v1","ok":false,"decision":"fail","reasons":["security.status_not_clean"],"slug":"outlook-inbox","displayName":"Outlook Inbox","pageUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/hith3sh/skills/outlook-inbox","publisherHandle":"hith3sh","publisherDisplayName":"Jay","publisherProfileUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/hith3sh","version":"1.0.5","resolvedFrom":"version","tag":null,"createdAt":1780989288306,"card":{"available":true,"path":"skill-card.md","url":"https://wry-manatee-359.convex.site/api/v1/skills/outlook-inbox/card?ownerHandle=hith3sh&version=1.0.5","sha256":"ff3cdd7ee92fb5bc5e76d120952038294be93e0cae6e203da0ec99aae3577e9f","size":2965,"contentType":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8"},"artifact":{"sourceFingerprint":"5aa5afc3e8f672c3f9ce83ed2079d9a48f3e03a6bd7f81c2c4092a958371fb01","bundleFingerprints":["4f84781851499ebf15dbf1d0627a6c6a47113de669f3e0fd64c1d59e2ab824d7","21851faee5ad8800e3e59b97fc026516c62678904b56a65d085c26ea551f36d1"],"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":15731,"sha256":"fe5393c40db76b5b5c15aca6529e4ca5530af85033798d0bf67f86e6ab0e886a","contentType":"text/markdown"}]},"provenance":{"source":"unavailable","reason":"No server-resolved GitHub import provenance is stored for this version."},"security":{"status":"suspicious","passed":false,"rawStatus":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"The skill has a coherent Outlook purpose, but it asks users to install and authorize a third-party ClawLink plugin with broad Microsoft account access without a pinned or verifiable plugin version.","model":null,"checkedAt":1789546310587,"scannerReports":{"aig":{"version":"2.1.0","$schema":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json","runs":[{"tool":{"driver":{"name":"aig-skill-scan","version":"0.2.1","informationUri":"https://github.com/Tencent/AI-Infra-Guard","rules":[{"id":"T01","name":"Skill Instruction Hijacking","shortDescription":{"text":"Alters the agent's current session goals or safety constraints when the skill is loaded"}},{"id":"T02","name":"Agent Memory Poisoning","shortDescription":{"text":"Writes attacker-controlled rules into persistent memory so they continue to affect future sessions"}},{"id":"T03","name":"Remote Payload Retrieval and Execution","shortDescription":{"text":"Fetches code from an external URL, allowing the effective payload to change after skill review"}},{"id":"T04","name":"Embedded Malicious Code","shortDescription":{"text":"Ships malicious scripts inside the skill package and executes them locally when invoked"}},{"id":"T05","name":"Unauthorized Access and Privilege Escalation","shortDescription":{"text":"Breaks least-privilege boundaries by obtaining permissions beyond the task's legitimate needs"}},{"id":"T06","name":"System Persistence","shortDescription":{"text":"Installs cross-session backdoors, hooks, services, or scheduled tasks that survive the skill run"}},{"id":"T07","name":"Tool Hijacking and Spoofing","shortDescription":{"text":"Modifies, wraps, spoofs, or replaces tools so legitimate-looking calls execute attacker logic"}},{"id":"T08","name":"Insecure Dependencies","shortDescription":{"text":"Introduces malicious packages or components through dependency confusion, typosquatting, or unsafe sources"}},{"id":"T09","name":"Insecure Skill Coding Practices","shortDescription":{"text":"Exposes exploitable flaws such as hardcoded secrets, command injection, plaintext sensitive data, or unsafe temp files"}},{"id":"other","name":"Other Risk","shortDescription":{"text":"Security issues not covered by the T01-T09 classification"}}]}},"results":[{"ruleId":"T08","level":"error","message":{"text":"Unpinned Third-Party Plugin with Broad OAuth-Backed Account Access"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"SKILL.md"},"region":{"startLine":47,"endLine":49}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"6d04a97d35908f68"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n**File Location**: `SKILL.md`, lines 47–49; supporting trust-boundary details at lines 12, 71, and 77  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Unpinned third-party dependency and supply-chain exposure  \n**Risk Level**: High\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```bash\nopenclaw plugins install clawhub:clawlink-plugin\nopenclaw config set tools.alsoAllow '[\"clawlink-plugin\"]' --strict-json\nopenclaw gateway restart\n```\n\nThe associated OAuth architecture is described at line 71:\n\n```text\nNo API key is required in chat. ClawLink stores the OAuth token securely and injects it into every Microsoft Graph request on the user's behalf.\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nThe skill directs the user to install `clawlink-plugin` from a mutable package identifier without specifying an audited version, immutable digest, signature, checksum, or other integrity constraint. It then adds the plugin to OpenClaw's tool allowlist and restarts the gateway, causing the installed third-party component to become active.\n\nThe plugin is subsequently entrusted with Microsoft OAuth-backed access and proxies requests to Microsoft Graph. The documented capabilities include reading Outlook messages and performing sensitive write operations such as sending email, deleting messages, modifying contacts, and creating, updating, or cancelling calendar events.\n\nThis creates a significant supply-chain trust boundary: the audited skill contains only instructions and does not include the plugin implementation. Consequently, the behavior of the component enabled by these commands cannot be verified from the project. If the package, publisher account, distribution infrastructure, update channel, or hosted service were compromised, a modified plugin could be delivered under the same mutable package name.\n\nThe skill appropriately requires previews and explicit confirmation for write operations at lines 109–112 and 292–296. However, those controls depend on correct enforcement by the external plugin and therefore do not fully mitigate compromise of the component responsible for implementing them.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. An attacker compromises the plugin publisher account, package registry, distribution infrastructure, update channel, or another component capable of replacing the mutable `clawhub:clawlink-plugin` package.\n2. The attacker publishes a modified plugin under the expected package identifier.\n3. A user follows the skill instructions and installs the package without a pinned version or verified digest.\n4. The configuration command explicitly authorizes the plugin's tools, and the gateway restart loads the compromised component.\n5. The user pairs the plugin and connects an Outlook account through the documented hosted OAuth flow.\n6. The compromised component receives or can exercise OAuth-backed Microsoft Graph capabilities.\n7. It abuses those capabilities within the granted scopes, potentially bypassing or misrepresenting preview and confirmation behavior.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nA successful compromise could expose Outlook data available under the granted Microsoft Graph scopes, including email content, message metadata, attachments, calendar events, contacts, and tasks.\n\nDepending on the granted permissions, the malicious component could also:\n\n- Send email or replies as the connected user.\n- Delete, move, copy, or modify mailbox resources.\n- Create, update, cancel, accept, or decline calendar events.\n- Create, update, or delete contacts.\n- Add attachments or create upload sessions.\n- Create mailbox rules that redirect, conceal, or manipulate future messages.\n- Abuse the user's identity for phishing or business-email-compromise activity.\n\nThe obtainable privileges are bounded by the connected Microsoft account, the OAuth scopes granted to ClawLink, Microsoft Graph authorization controls, and any organizational policies. The reviewed file does not establish that the current provider or plugin is malicious; the risk arises from installing and authorizing an unaudited, unpinned third-party component with sensitive account access.\n]]>","severity":"High"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Pin the plugin to a reviewed, immutable version rather than installing a mutable package identifier.\n2. Verify an authenticated package signature and cryptographic digest before installation.\n3. Publish or vendor the exact plugin source associated with the pinned artifact so its implementation can be independently audited.\n4. Document the precise Microsoft Graph delegated and application permissions requested by the OAuth flow.\n5. Apply least-privilege scopes and avoid broad mailbox, contacts, calendar, or offline-access permissions unless required.\n6. Require explicit reauthorization whenever requested OAuth scopes expand.\n7. Enforce previews and user confirmation outside the third-party plugin where possible, particularly for sending, deletion, mailbox-rule changes, and calendar operations.\n8. Isolate OAuth tokens from unnecessary plugin code, encrypt them at rest, restrict token access, and avoid logging credentials or sensitive message content.\n9. Provide users with procedures to inspect connected applications, revoke Microsoft consent, terminate ClawLink sessions, and remove the plugin.\n10. Establish a controlled update process that validates every new release before deployment instead of automatically trusting the latest package.\n11. Document package provenance, publisher identity, security contacts, incident-response procedures, and the hosted service's token retention and revocation policies.\n]]>"}}]}],"properties":{"securityScore":60,"primaryLanguage":"Markdown","llm":"gpt-5.6","startTime":1789546228.312255,"endTime":1789546276.350385}}]},"skillspector":{"skill":{"name":"outlook-inbox","source":"/tmp/clawhub-codex-scan-v57d3hr614td8hvrcbxnfvqj398egyxq-a8fTx4/clawscan-skillspector-1476648375/artifact","scanned_at":"2026-09-16T08:10:21.778583+00:00"},"risk_assessment":{"score":31,"severity":"MEDIUM","recommendation":"CAUTION","max_issue_severity":"HIGH"},"components":[{"path":"SKILL.md","type":"markdown","lines":358,"executable":false,"size_bytes":15731,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null}],"structured_summaries":[],"issues":[{"id":"E4","finding_id":"finding-8db9342b5c8a497b9554499b17d452cd","category":"Data Exfiltration","pattern":"Context Leakage","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":172,"end_line":null},"finding":"upload session ","explanation":"Code or instructions that leak agent conversation context to external services, potentially exposing sensitive user interactions.","remediation":"Remove any code that sends prompts, responses, or session data externally. Preserve user privacy; never exfiltrate conversation content.","code_snippet":"| Tool | Description | Mode |\n|------|-------------|------|\n| `outlook_add_mail_attachment` | Add an attachment to a message | Write |\n| `outlook_create_attachment_upload_session` | Create an upload session for large attachments (>3 MB) | Write |\n| `outlook_list_attachments` | List attachments on a message | Read |\n\n### Rules & Categories","intent":null,"tags":["Data Exfiltration","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"8ad0caf4ec790d4283809de8c2d22de46dc4eb80f928dded6f8ac0f0c3b47a83","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":172,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"E4","finding_id":"finding-8db9342b5c8a497b9554499b17d452cd","category":"Data Exfiltration","pattern":"Context Leakage","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":302,"end_line":null},"finding":"upload session ","explanation":"Code or instructions that leak agent conversation context to external services, potentially exposing sensitive user interactions.","remediation":"Remove any code that sends prompts, responses, or session data externally. Preserve user privacy; never exfiltrate conversation content.","code_snippet":